  "major": "creative-writing-bfa-nyc-2e1034ad",
  "major_name": "Creative Writing",
  "degree": "BFA",
  "campus": "New York",
  "college": "College of Social Sciences and Humanities",
  "overview_text": "The Bachelor of Fine Arts in Creative Writing program provides students the opportunity to pursue the study and practice of poetry, fiction, and creative nonfiction in a comprehensive fashion. Building on the foundations of English and world literature, students explore the critical traditions that shape literary history and study how to analyze the written criticism of contemporary writers in order to discuss their most favored methods. Students in the creative writing program investigate their own writing in a self-reflective manner while focusing on the importance of redrafting. Through these processes, students evaluate the poetry, fiction, and creative nonfiction of their classmates and articulate that appraisal through the workshop model. This program emphasizes both the art and craft of writing, encouraging students to refine their creative voice while building a solid foundation in the theory, history, and criticism of literature. This program is designed to support both emerging and experienced writers by providing a supportive and dynamic creative community, where students can collaborate, receive mentorship, and prepare for a variety of writing careers in today’s evolving literary landscape. This program is currently only available to former Marymount Manhattan College students who are eligible for continued enrollment at Northeastern University. This program includes required courses that will be taught on ground at the Northeastern New York City campus.",
  "source_text": "Complete all courses listed below unless otherwise indicated. Also complete any corequisite labs, recitations, clinicals, or tools courses where specified and complete any additional courses needed beyond specific college and major requirements to satisfy graduation credit requirements.\nUniversitywide Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nUniversitywide Requirements\n.\nNUpath Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nNUpath Requirements\n.\nNew York State Liberal Arts and Sciences Requirements\nAll undergraduate students enrolled in programs at the New York campus are required to fulfill\nNew York State Liberal Arts and Sciences requirements\n.\nMajor Required Courses\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nENGL 1140\nGrammar: The Architecture of English\n4\nENGL 1400\nIntroduction to Literary Studies\n4\nENGL 1450\nReading and Writing in the Digital Age\n4\nENGL 2700\nCreative Writing\n4\nENGL 2750\nCreative Writing 2\n4\nENGL 2830\nLiterary Theory\n4\nENGL 3376\nCreative Nonfiction\n4\nENGL 3377\nPoetry Workshop\n4\nENGL 3378\nFiction Workshop\n4\nENGL 3380\nWriting Seminar\n4\nENGL 3382\nPublishing in the 21st Century\n4\nENGL 3465\nCreative Writing 3\n4\nENGL 3710\nMajor Seminar\n4\nENGL 4710\nCapstone Seminar\n4\nEnglish Elective\nComplete 4 semester hours of ENGL coursework within the 1001-5999 course number range, not used to fulfill other requirements of this program.\n4\nGeneral Electives/NUpath\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nComplete any remaining semester hours after major to fulfill NUpath courses and to reach the minimum semester-hour program requirement.\nMajor Credit Requirement\nComplete 60 semester hours in the major.\nProgram Requirement\n128 total semester hours required",
